<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><BaseHead title="Privacy — Paisa Inbox" description="How Paisa Inbox handles financial information, Account Aggregator consent, retention, and your controls." /></head><body><main class="legal-page"><a href="/">← Back to Paisa Inbox</a><span class="eyebrow">PRIVACY NOTICE · BETA</span><h1>Your financial context belongs to you.</h1><p class="lead">Paisa Inbox uses financial information only to provide your private ledger, reviews, budgets, and insights. It does not sell financial information or use it for advertising.</p>
<div class="legal-callout"><strong>Commercial-launch prerequisite</strong><p>The operating entity’s legal name, registered address, privacy contact, grievance contact, hosting locations, and final retention schedule must be inserted and reviewed by Indian counsel before public onboarding. This beta notice describes the product’s implemented behaviour; it is not a substitute for that review.</p></div>
<h2>Information Paisa processes</h2><p>Account identity and email supplied by the private sign-in layer; transaction amount, merchant narration, time, category, account label, and review context; payment-account and loan details you add; monthly plans and reflections; consent status and masked account details; device sessions; reminder preferences; and limited security and consent audit events.</p><p>When you start an Account Aggregator connection, your bank-registered mobile number is sent to Setu to begin account discovery and consent. Paisa stores only its last four digits. Paisa does not ask for or store a net-banking password, PIN, or OTP.</p>
<h2>Why it is processed</h2><p>To import and reconcile records, prevent duplicates, maintain your review queue, prepare budgets and reports, sync approved devices, send requested reminders, secure the service, and honour export, revocation, and deletion requests. Bank data requested through AA uses ReBIT purpose code 102: customer spending patterns, budget, or other reporting.</p>
<h2 id="account-aggregator">Account Aggregator roles and consent</h2><p>Paisa is the Financial Information User (FIU) that defines the request and receives data you approve. Setu provides the AA gateway and technology integration; the regulated Account Aggregator named on the hosted consent screen manages the consent journey; your bank is the Financial Information Provider. Consent states the data types, history range, purpose, retrieval frequency, processing life, and expiry.</p><p>Current product defaults request deposit summaries and transactions for up to the previous 12 months, at most once daily, for a consent lasting up to 12 months. The app does not request deposit-account profile data. You may reject the request and continue using statement import.</p>
<h2>Sharing and processors</h2><p>Information is shared only with service providers needed for private hosting, authentication, Account Aggregator connectivity, and requested device notifications, under the final operator’s contracts and instructions. Raw PDF and CSV files are parsed in your browser; only transaction rows you confirm are uploaded. No transaction data is currently sent to OpenAI or ChatGPT.</p>
<h2>Retention and deletion</h2><p>Financial records remain while your account is active and they are needed for the service, subject to the final legal retention schedule. AA consent metadata and security logs may be retained where needed to demonstrate consent, prevent fraud, meet legal obligations, or resolve disputes. Selecting “Delete account” first attempts to revoke every current AA consent, then deletes the Paisa account. Deleting transactions requires you to disconnect current bank consent first so a later sync cannot restore them.</p>
<h2 id="your-rights">Your controls and rights</h2><p>You can view and revoke AA consents under Bank connections, export your data from Preferences, correct transaction details, disconnect devices, delete financial records, or delete the account. Consent withdrawal stops future consent-based retrieval; it does not rewrite records your bank is legally required to retain. The final launch notice must provide a working privacy and grievance contact through which you can request access, correction, erasure, or grievance resolution and, where applicable, approach the Data Protection Board of India.</p>
<h2>Security and incidents</h2><p>Queries are scoped to the authenticated user, provider credentials remain server-side secrets, webhook requests require verification, duplicate events are ignored, sensitive responses are not cached, and consent actions are auditable. No system is risk-free. The operator will maintain an incident-response and legally required notification process before public launch.</p>
<h2>Children and changes</h2><p>Paisa is not designed for children and should not be used to connect a child’s financial account without a legally reviewed, verifiable-parental-consent flow. Material notice changes should be shown in-product with an updated effective date.</p>
<p class="legal-note">Last updated: 17 August 2026. Product behaviour and legal readiness are separate gates; public onboarding remains blocked until operator details, contracts, provider onboarding, and counsel review are complete.</p></main></body></html>
